# PART 11: ANIMATION ENGINE

This is one of the most important modules.

Create a unified animation engine with multiple providers.

### Mode A: Generative image-to-video

When a suitable model is available, generate short video clips from reference images and motion prompts.

Input:
- Reference image.
- Motion description.
- Camera movement.
- Duration.
- FPS.
- Model settings.

Output:
- Video clip.
- Generation metadata.
- Error status.

### Mode B: Generative text-to-video

When supported, generate clips from text prompts.

### Mode C: Deterministic image animation

This mode must work without a generative video model.

Implement:

1. Slow zoom-in.
2. Slow zoom-out.
3. Horizontal pan.
4. Vertical pan.
5. Camera shake.
6. Parallax movement.
7. Foreground and background separation.
8. Particle effects.
9. Rain.
10. Snow.
11. Smoke.
12. Fire.
13. Lightning.
14. Fog.
15. Light rays.
16. Glowing objects.
17. Fade transitions.
18. Dissolve transitions.
19. Motion blur where practical.
20. Animated masks and cutouts.

Use FFmpeg, OpenCV, MoviePy or another suitable engine.

### Mode D: Hybrid animation

Combine:
- AI-generated clips.
- Animated still images.
- Layered motion graphics.
- Particle effects.
- Camera movements.

Automatically choose the method based on the shot's requirements and available resources.

Do not force every shot through a generative video model.

### Animation instruction parser

Convert user-provided natural-language instructions into a structured motion specification.

For example:

"Camera slowly moves toward Mahishasura while lightning flashes in the background."

Convert this into structured parameters:

- Camera: push-in.
- Camera speed: slow.
- Subject motion: minimal.
- Environment: moving storm clouds.
- Effect: lightning flash.
- Duration: 8 seconds.
- Preserve character identity: true.

If automatic interpretation fails, allow manual configuration.

